82: Learn more\nL83: cite23†Gemini for Science†ai.google cite24†Experimental Tools†labs.google cite25†Science Skills†antigravity.google L84: \nL85: About\nL86: ## Our mission is to build AI responsibly to benefit humanity\nL87: \nL88: cite26†About Google DeepMind L89: \nL90: Learn more\nL91: \nL92: cite29†Education cite30†Our National Partnerships for AI cite31†Accelerator programs cite32†The Podcast cite33†DeepMind Institute†institute.deepmind.com L93: \nL94: cite15†Responsibility Ensuring AI safety through proactive security, even against evolving threats cite27†News Discover our latest AI breakthroughs, projects, and updates cite28†Careers We’re looking for people who want to make a real, positive impact on the world L95: cite34†Build with Gemini†aistudio.google.com cite35†Try Gemini†gemini.google.com L96: \nL97: cite36†Google DeepMind L98: \nL99: cite37†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite38†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI cite39†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google cite40†Google Research Explore our research†research.google L100: \nL101: Products and apps\nL102: cite35†Gemini app Chat with Gemini†gemini.google.com cite34†Google AI Studio Build with our next-gen AI models†aistudio.google.com cite41†Google Antigravity Our agentic development platform†antigravity.google L103: \nL104: cite0†Models L105: \nL106: cite10†Research L107: \nL108: cite17†Science L109: \nL110: cite26†About L111: \nL112: cite34†Build with Gemini†aistudio.google.com cite35†Try Gemini†gemini.google.com L113: \nL114: November 11, 2025 Research\nL115: # Teaching AI to see the world more like we do\nL116: \nL117: Andrew Lampinen, Klaus Greff\nL118: \nL119: cite42†Image: A conceptual diagram illustrating AI and human alignment. Nine images (e.g., a giraffe, a car, and a tent) are organised near colourful dot clusters, with lines grouping related concepts, such as a giraffe with a sea turtle and a car with an airplane.†lh3.googleusercontent.com L120: \nL121: Your browser does not support the audio element.\nL122: \nL123: Listen to article 10 minutes\nL124: New research shows that reorganizing a model’s visual representations can make it more helpful, robust and reliable\nL125: “Visual” artificial intelligence (AI) is everywhere. We use it to sort our photos, identify unknown flowers and steer our cars. But these powerful systems do not always “see” the world as we do, and they sometimes behave in surprising ways. For example, an AI system that can identify hundreds of car manufacturers and models might still fail to capture the commonalities between a car and an airplane, i.e. both are large vehicles made primarily of metal.\nL126: To better understand these differences, today we’re publishing a cite43†new paper in Nature†www.nature.com analyzing the important ways AI systems organize the visual world differently from humans. We present a method for better aligning these systems with human knowledge, and show that addressing these discrepancies improves their robustness and ability to generalize.\nL127: \nL128: This work is a step towards building more intuitive and trustworthy AI systems.\nL129: ## Why AI struggles with the “odd one out”\nL130: \nL131: When you see a cat, your brain creates a mental representation that captures everything about the cat, from basic concepts like its color and furriness to high-level concepts like its \"cat-ness.\" AI vision models also produce representations, by mapping images to points in a high-dimensional space where similar items (like two sheep) are placed close together, and different ones (a sheep and a cake) are far apart.\nL132: To understand the differences in how human and model representations are organized, we used the classic \"odd-one-out\" task from cognitive science, asking both humans and models to pick which of three given images does not fit in with the others. This test reveals which two items they \"see\" as most similar.\nL133: Sometimes, everyone agrees. Given a tapir, a sheep, and a birthday cake, both humans and models reliably pick the cake as the odd one out. Other times, the right answer is unclear, and people and models disagree.\nL134: Interestingly, we also found many cases where humans strongly agree on an answer, but the AI models get it wrong. For the third example below, most people agree the starfish is the odd one out. But most vision models focus more on superficial features like background color and texture, and choose the cat instead.\nL135: cite44†Image: Three rows of \"odd-one-out\" image triplets comparing human and AI classifications. The top row shows a tapir, sheep, and a birthday cake, labeled \"Aligned\" as both humans and AI select the cake. The middle row shows lemons, a red telephone booth, and a gazelle, labeled \"Unclear\" as human judgments vary.\nL136: The bottom row shows a fox, a starfish, and a cat, labeled \"Unaligned\" because humans select the starfish as the odd one out, whereas the AI incorrectly selects the cat.†lh3.googleusercontent.com L137: Three examples of the \"odd one out\" task. Three images of subjects in the natural world are shown in three rows. The first row shows an easy task where humans and models align. The second row shows an example where humans and AI models disagree. The third row shows an example where humans tend to agree, but models make a different choice.\nL138: This example illustrates a systematic misalignment between humans and AI, which we observed across many different vision models, from image classifiers to unsupervised models.\nL139: \nL140: The overall problem can be seen in a two-dimensional projection (PCA) of an AI’s internal map.\nL141: Below, on the left, we show a vision model's internal map which appears unstructured, with representations for different categories like animals, food, and furniture all mixed together. The structure on the right is the improved representation map after we applied our alignment method where categories are clearly organized.\nL142: 【45†Image: Two scatter plots comparing the internal representation maps of an unaligned and aligned classifier. The \"Unaligned classifier\" plot on the left shows a highly mixed, chaotic jumble of colored data points representing different categories, with images of a buffalo, a spider, and grass clustered close together.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDS-STAR: A state-of-the-art versatile data science agent (https://research.google/blog/ds-star-a-state-of-the-art-versatile-data-science-agent/)\nciteturn2322view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2320view2\",\"pattern\":\"November\"}); Total lines: 184\nL38: ## Explore our many areas of focus\nL39: \nL40: cite1†Explore all research areas L41: \nL42: Applied AI & sciences\nL43: \nL44: cite2†Earth AI cite3†Health AI cite4†Science AI cite5†Sustainability & crisis resilience L45: \nL46: Foundational ML & algorithms\nL47: \nL48: cite6†Algorithms & theory cite7†Information retrieval cite8†Machine intelligence cite9†Machine perception cite10†Natural language processing L49: \nL50: People, systems & quantum AI\nL51: cite11†Human-computer interaction and visualization cite12†Networking cite13†Quantum AI cite14†Responsible AI cite15†Anti abuse cite16†Software engineering cite17†Software systems L52: \nL53: Learn More\nL54: \nL55: cite18†Publications cite19†Projects L56: \nL57: Resources\nL58: ## Building a collaborative ecosystem\nL59: \nL60: cite20†Datasets Access high-quality datasets to accelerate your research. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L61: \nL62: cite30†Conferences & events L63: \nL64: Careers\nL65: ## Shaping the future together\nL66: \nL67: cite23†See all programs L68: \nL69: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L70: \nL71: cite31†Blog L72: \nL73: About\nL74: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL75: \nL76: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L77: \nL78: cite32†Google Research L79: \nL80: cite33†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite34†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI†deepmind.google cite35†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google L81: \nL82: [Button: Research ]\nL83: \nL84: [Button: Resources ]\nL85: \nL86: cite30†Conferences & events L87: [Button: Careers ]\nL88: \nL89: cite31†Blog L90: \nL91: [Button: About ]\nL92: \nL93: [Input: Search]\nL94: \nL95: Search\nL96: \nL97: cite36†Image: DS-STAR: A state-of-the-art versatile data science agent†storage.googleapis.com L98: # DS-STAR: A state-of-the-art versatile data science agent\nL99: \nL100: November 6, 2025\nL101: \nL102: Jinsung Yoon, Research Scientist, and Jaehyun Nam, Student Researcher, Google Cloud\nL103: \nL104: DS-STAR is a state-of-the-art data science agent whose versatility is shown by its ability to automate a range of tasks — from statistical analysis to visualization and data wrangling — across various data types, culminating in a top-ranking performance on the famous DABStep benchmark.\nL105: ## Quick links\nL106: \nL107:   * cite37†Paper†arxiv.org L108: Data science is a field dedicated to transforming raw data into meaningful, actionable insights, playing an essential role in solving real-world challenges. Businesses often depend on cite38†data-driven insights†link.springer.com to make pivotal strategic decisions. However, the data science process is frequently complex, demanding a high level of expertise in fields like computer science and statistics.\nL109: This workflow consists of many time-intensive activities, from interpreting various documents to performing complex data processing and statistical analysis.\nL110: To streamline this complex workflow, cite39†recent research†arxiv.org has focused on using off-the-shelf large language models (LLMs) to create autonomous data science agents. The goal of these agents is to convert natural language questions into executable code for a desired task. But despite making significant cite40†progress†arxiv.org , current data science agents have several limitations that hinder their practical use.\nL111: A major issue is their heavy reliance on well-structured data, like CSV files in relational databases. This limited focus ignores the valuable information contained in the diverse and cite41†heterogeneous data formats†huggingface.co , such as JSON, unstructured text, and markdown files, that are common in real-world applications. Another challenge is that many cite42†data science problems†arxiv.org are open-ended and lack ground-truth labels, making it difficult to verify if an agent's reasoning is correct.\nL112: cite43†Image: DS-STAR -1†storage.googleapis.com L113: \nL114: Data science agents answer user queries by generating code that operates on diverse data formats. Following the code's execution, the agent provides a final solution, which may take the form of a trained model, a processed database, a visualization, or a text-formatted answer.\nL115: To that end, we present cite37†DS-STAR†arxiv.org , a new agent designed to solve data science problems. DS-STAR introduces three key innovations: (1) a data file analysis module that automatically extracts context from varied data formats, including unstructured ones; (2) a verification stage where an LLM-based judge assesses the plan’s sufficiency at each step; and (3) a sequential planning process that iteratively refines the initial plan based on feedback.\nL116: This iterative refinement allows DS-STAR to handle complex analyses that draw verifiable insights from multiple data sources. We demonstrate that DS-STAR achieves state-of-the-art performance on challenging benchmarks like cite44†DABStep†arxiv.org , cite42†KramaBench†arxiv.org , and cite45†DA-Code†arxiv.org . It especially excels with tasks involving diverse, heterogeneous data files.\nL117: ## DS-STAR\nL118: \nL119: The DS-STAR framework operates in two main stages. First, it automatically examines all files in a directory and creates a textual summary of their structure and contents. This summary becomes a vital source of context for tackling the task at hand.\nL120: \nL121: cite46†Image: DS-STAR -2†storage.googleapis.com L122: \nL123: DS-STAR creates a Python script to analyze diverse data files by extracting key information.\nL124: Second, DS-STAR engages in a primary loop of planning, implementing, and verifying. The Planner agent first creates a high-level plan, which the Coder agent then transforms into a code script. Subsequently, the Verifier agent evaluates the code's effectiveness in solving the problem. The Verifier agent is an LLM-based judge prompted to determine if the current plan is adequate.\nL125: If the judge finds the plan insufficient, DS-STAR refines it by altering or adding steps (determined by the Router agent) and then repeats the cycle. Importantly, DS-STAR uses a method that mimics how an expert analyst uses tools like cite47†Google colab†colab.sandbox.google.com to build a plan sequentially, reviewing intermediate results before proceeding.\nL126: This iterative cycle continues until a plan is deemed satisfactory or the maximum number of rounds (10) is reached, at which point the final code is delivered as the solution.\nL127: cite48†Image: DS-STAR -3†storage.googleapis.com L128: \nL129: DS-STAR's workflow is an iterative loop. It starts by executing a simple plan and uses a Verifier agent to check if it's sufficient. If the plan is inadequate, a Router agent guides the refinement by adding a step or correcting any errors before the cycle repeats. The process continues until the Verifier approves the plan or the maximum number of rounds is reached.\nL130: ## Evaluation\nL131: \n"